---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="permissions">

          <p>
            Every tool action is checked against a ruleset whose effect is <strong>allow</strong>, <strong>ask</strong> or <strong>deny</strong>. Rules from the agent, from the repository and from your saved answers are merged: deny wins, then ask, then allow, and anything with no matching rule defaults to ask. An agent with no permissions at all is denied everything rather than trusted.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you answer <strong>always</strong>, that allow is saved for the repository, so the same action is not asked again. Saved answers are per repository, and you can clear them.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            For unattended runs the terminal agent takes <code>--auto</code> (also spelled <code>--yolo</code> or <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code>). It auto-approves anything not explicitly denied — deny rules still hold. It is off by default, and it is the flag to think twice about.
          </p>

          <h2 id="project-trust">Repository trust</h2>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, a repository's own <code>vector.json</code> and <code>.vector</code> folder (its
            agents, commands, modes, plugins, tools, skills, MCP servers, formatters and language servers) load only
            after you trust the repository, because they can run code on your computer. Until then Vector applies the
            repository's deny rules, and its run limits, loop pauses and sharing settings only where they are stricter
            than yours, so a cloned repository cannot raise your spend cap or switch loop pauses off. From desktop
            1.99.109, a repository's run limits are ignored even once you trust it, because spend limits are one
            overall set of yours, and Vector says so once for each repository file that still sets <code>budget.run</code>,
            not again for the repository's agent worktrees or copies.
          </p>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.109, the app asks once, the first time you open a repository that has settings of its
            own: <strong>Trust this repository?</strong> Choose <strong>Trust this repository</strong> to load its
            settings as they are now, or <strong>Not now</strong> to leave them off, and the app does not ask about it
            again. Every such repository asks, including one you clone with <strong>Open from GitHub</strong>, because
            a clone can come from anyone's link. To trust one later, choose <strong>Trust this repository…</strong> in
            its menu on Home, or open Settings, <strong>Repository trust</strong>. Only you can trust a repository on
            your computer, so a guest you invite is never asked; the app tells the guest that you decide. In desktop
            1.99.108 the same approval is the button in the session header. The TUI's <code>/trust</code> and the
            terminal's <code>vector trust status</code> and <code>vector trust approve</code> arrive with the next CLI
            release; the CLI that npm and <code>vectordev.ai/install</code> serve today has neither, and Windows WSL
            servers keep the 1.99.99 engine, without repository trust, until a later desktop release requires that CLI.
          </p>
          <p>
            The language servers and formatters you turn on in your own settings also stay off in a repository you have
            not trusted, because they run its code, and your own local MCP servers start in an empty folder outside it,
            so the repository's <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.npmrc</code> or Python modules cannot take them over.
            This holds in a repository with no settings of its own too. The app does not ask about such a repository;
            while your tools are held back there it shows a <strong>Repository not trusted</strong> notice, and you
            trust it in Settings, <strong>Repository trust</strong>. Agent and parallel workspaces are folders of their
            own with their own trust: the question about one reads <strong>Trust this workspace?</strong>, and from one
            of their sessions Settings shows that folder beside the repository it came from.
          </p>
          <p>
            Trust covers the repository as it was when you approved it. A later change to anything that can run, from a
            pull or an edit, needs approval again, and the app asks once more; plans, memory and rules that Vector
            writes into <code>.vector</code> do not. Integrations you add from the app are kept with your own settings
            rather than in the repository, so they stay after a restart. A trusted repository can add allow rules, but
            it cannot lift a deny in your own settings.
          </p>

          <h2 id="prompt-files">Prompt files</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} an edit to a file Vector reads into an agent's instructions or permissions asks
            <code>prompt_source</code> every time, whatever your rules, an earlier answer or <code>--auto</code> would
            allow. These are the config files Vector loaded (your global <code>vector.json</code>, and a trusted
            repository's own), the agents, modes, commands, skills and plugins in a config folder such as
            <code>~/.config/vector</code>, <code>~/.vector</code> (even before it exists) or a trusted repository's
            <code>.vector</code>, a file a loaded config's prompt settings name with <code>{"{file:...}"}</code>, the
            skills in <code>~/.claude/skills</code>, <code>~/.agents/skills</code> and your <code>skills.paths</code>,
            your global <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, and instruction files outside the
            repository. Vector asks before its edit, write and patch tools change one, and before a shell redirect or
            one of the common write commands (<code>cp</code>, <code>mv</code>, <code>install</code>, <code>ln</code>,
            <code>tee</code>, <code>sed -i</code> and <code>dd</code>, and on Windows PowerShell's
            <code>Copy-Item</code>, <code>Move-Item</code> and <code>Tee-Object</code> and cmd's <code>copy</code> and
            <code>move</code>) does, a target spelled with
            <code>$HOME</code> or <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code> included. A repository's own <code>AGENTS.md</code>
            is not one: you often ask the agent to edit it, and the change shows in <code>git diff</code>. A
            <code>vector.json</code> that Vector does not load, such as a test fixture, is edited like any other file,
            so an unattended run that changes fixtures is not stopped.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a prompt file outside the repository that changed since you last approved it is held
            when Vector next starts: a held agent, command, skill, plugin or instruction file is not loaded (a changed
            module beside the plugins of a folder holds all of them), and a held global config keeps its providers and
            sign-ins but not its prompt settings (agent and mode prompts, commands, instructions, plugins and skills),
            nor does it when a file those settings name with <code>{"{file:...}"}</code> changed. The first start
            records your files as they are; after that, a folder Vector has not seen before, such as a new
            <code>~/.vector</code>, is held until you approve it. An edit you approved when Vector asked is not held.
            The app shows the held files in a banner with <strong>Approve</strong>, which approves only the files it
            listed, as they were listed: if one changed since, nothing is approved and the banner reads them again. In
            a terminal outside Vector, <code>vector trust prompts</code> lists them and
            <code>vector trust prompts approve</code> approves them and names each one. Prompt files inside a
            repository are left to repository trust. <code>vector trust prompts approve</code> and
            <code>vector trust approve</code> refuse to run from an agent's shell or from Vector's own terminal, and
            the shell asks <code>agent_marker</code> every time before a command that removes the marker they check
            (<code>unset</code>, <code>env -u</code>, <code>env -i</code>). This stops an agent approving what it wrote
            by accident or on a page's say-so; it is not a boundary on its own.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            {release113.since} this is an ask and a hold, not a guarantee. Vector does not see what an interpreter
            (<code>node -e</code>, <code>python -c</code>, <code>bash -c</code>), <code>git apply</code>,
            <code>patch</code>, the V2 bash tool or an MCP filesystem server writes, so those writes do not ask. The
            hold catches what they change outside the repository at the next start, and repository trust what they
            change inside it. A program that can write Vector's state folder can also change what you last approved,
            and one that clears its environment some way the shell does not see can run the owner-only commands; only
            the opt-in shell sandbox (<code>VECTOR_SHELL_SANDBOX</code>) is a boundary.
          </p>

          <h2 id="learned-skills">Learned skills</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} loading a learned skill asks <code>skill</code>, as loading any skill does. A skill
            Vector approved on its own, after two matching successful runs, also asks <code>learned_skill</code>, which
            defaults to ask; a skill you approved does not. Every command in a learned skill still goes through your
            permission rules.
          </p>

          <h2 id="read-only-specialists">Read-only specialists</h2>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, the read-only specialists (explore, review and security) ask before they run a test
            or build command such as <code>bun test</code>, <code>npm test</code>, <code>pytest</code>,
            <code>cargo test</code> or <code>go test</code>, because those run the repository's own code. Approve once,
            or approve always for the rest of the session. In an unattended <code>vector run</code> they are refused
            unless you pass <code>--auto</code>, and git and pull request reads still need no approval. The terminal
            agent gets this with the next CLI release, and Windows WSL servers once a later desktop release requires
            that CLI.
          </p>

          <h2 id="private-network">Your own network</h2>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, when an agent's web fetch would reach your own computer or network (a dev server on
            <code>localhost</code>, an intranet page, a cloud metadata address), Vector asks for
            <code>private_network</code> as well as <code>webfetch</code>, so a hostile page cannot steer the agent
            into your network. Approve it once for that address, or allow it in your settings, for example
            <code>"permission": {"{"} "private_network": {"{"} "http://localhost:3000": "allow" {"}"} {"}"}</code>.
            Addresses you put in your own settings (remote instructions, skill catalogs, providers such as a LAN Ollama
            host, MCP servers, references) are reached without asking. The terminal agent gets this with the next CLI
            release, and Windows WSL servers once a later desktop release requires that CLI.
          </p>

          <h2 id="shell-sandbox">The shell sandbox</h2>
          <p>
            Commands an agent runs can be confined to their workspace by the operating system's own mechanism — a
            seatbelt profile on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux where it is installed — by setting
            <code>VECTOR_SHELL_SANDBOX</code> from 1.99.99. Releases before 1.99.99, such as desktop
            1.99.8 and npm CLI 1.99.7, do not read that name, so setting it there confines nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            It is opt-in and off by default, and Windows ships no equivalent, so on Windows Vector reports that it is
            running unconfined instead of implying protection it cannot provide.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
